steps.body:
Java has six comparison operators: `==` (equal), `!=` (not equal), `<`, `>`, `<=`, and `>=`. Each one produces a `boolean`, `true` or `false`, that you can print or store.

Inside a concatenation, wrap the comparison in parentheses. Without them, `+` runs first, so `"Adult: " + age >= 18` tries to compare the text `"Adult: 20"` with the number 18, which does not compile.

```java
int age = 20;
System.out.println("Adult: " + (age >= 18)); // Adult: true
```

An `if` runs its block only when the condition is `true`. Always use braces, even for one line, so a second line you add later is not silently left outside the `if`.

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int age = 20;
        {{code}}
    }
}
```

```java
if (age >= 18) {
    System.out.println("Can vote");
}
```

Two gotchas with doubles. Decimals are stored in binary, so `0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3` is `false`: never compare doubles with `==`. Check that their difference is tiny instead, as a later lesson shows. And `NaN` is not equal to anything, not even itself, so test it with `Double.isNaN(x)`.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int age = 15;
        int adultAge = 18;
        System.out.println("age == adultAge: " + (age == adultAge));
        System.out.println("age != adultAge: " + (age != adultAge));
        System.out.println("age < adultAge: " + (age < adultAge));
        System.out.println("age > adultAge: " + (age > adultAge));
        System.out.println("age <= adultAge: " + (age <= adultAge));
        System.out.println("age >= adultAge: " + (age >= adultAge));

        System.out.println("0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3: " + (0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3));
        double nan = 0.0 / 0;
        System.out.println("nan == nan: " + (nan == nan));
        System.out.println("Double.isNaN(nan): " + Double.isNaN(nan));

        int temperature = 34;
        if (temperature > 30) {
            System.out.println(temperature + " is hot");
        }
        if (temperature < 0) {
            System.out.println(temperature + " is freezing");
        }

        temperature = -3;
        if (temperature > 30) {
            System.out.println(temperature + " is hot");
        }
        if (temperature < 0) {
            System.out.println(temperature + " is freezing");
        }
    }
}

