steps.body:
A `boolean` variable is already a condition, so write `if (isReady)` and `if (!isReady)`, not `if (isReady == true)`. A typo like `if (isReady = true)` even compiles: it assigns `true` and the block always runs. Name flags like questions: `isOpen`, `hasKey`.

Reference variables such as `String` can hold `null`. Test for it with `x == null` or `x != null` before you use the value.

To compare the text inside two Strings, use `equals`. Never use `==` on String contents: it checks whether both variables point to the same object, and it can say `false` for equal text (the Strings lesson shows why).

```java
String role = "admin";
if (role.equals("admin")) {
    System.out.println("Full access");
}
```

Calling a method on `null` throws a `NullPointerException`, so `role.equals("admin")` crashes when `role` is `null`. Two safe forms:

- Guard first: `role != null && role.equals("admin")`. Short-circuiting skips `equals` when `role` is `null`.
- Put the literal first: `"admin".equals(role)` is simply `false` when `role` is `null`.

